# Yantrik — what starts with the desktop.
#
# labwc runs this once, after the compositor is up. Everything here is a service the desktop
# is expected to provide and did not: with none of it running, an application that posted a
# notification was talking to nobody, and an action needing authorisation failed with no
# prompt and no explanation.
#
# Each line is guarded by `command -v` so a machine missing one of these still gets the rest,
# rather than the file dying at the first absence and silently skipping what follows.

# ── The splash, until the shell draws ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# First, so it is up as soon as it can be. plymouth's last frame, the emblem on the Lake ground,
# is on the screen until labwc takes the display (boot-unlock/splash); labwc's own first frames
# would be black until the shell draws, 1-5 s on a slow machine. This puts the same picture back
# under it: the same image plymouth draws (boot-unlock/theme/emblem.png), at its own size,
# centred, on #0e1117. The shell is fullscreen over it, and setting a wallpaper replaces it
# (pkill swaybg).
SPLASH=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/yantrik/emblem.png
command -v swaybg >/dev/null && [ -f "$SPLASH" ] && swaybg -m center -c '#0e1117' -i "$SPLASH" >/dev/null 2>&1 &

# ── Notifications ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing is started here any more, and that is the fix rather than an omission.
#
# This line used to be `command -v mako >/dev/null && mako &`. mako held
# org.freedesktop.Notifications, drew popups in its own style, and kept them in its own head —
# so the shell's notification centre stayed empty all day on a machine that was showing
# notifications all day. Worse, the shell ALSO implemented that interface and claimed the same
# name from a thread of its own, so which of the two a `notify-send` reached came down to start
# order; the audit of 17 September caught mako winning by a few hundred milliseconds.
#
# The notifications service owns the name now. It is the machine's one notification store, it is
# autostarted by the shell, and the shell draws what it holds. `libnotify-bin` stays installed —
# that is only the `notify-send` client, and it now lands in the same store as everything else.
# See design/notifications-2026-09-21.md.

# ── Authorisation ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The polkit daemon was already running with no agent to show a prompt, so anything asking to
# be authorised was refused by default with nothing on screen to say so.
command -v lxpolkit >/dev/null && lxpolkit &

# ── Portals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# xdg-desktop-portal and its wlroots backend are D-Bus activated, so they do not need starting
# here — but they do need to know which session they are in, and that is read from the
# environment at activation time. yantrik-session exports XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP before labwc
# starts for exactly this reason.
